> to all equipment would be merely bizarre.

Well, no, the idea was to limit that effect to
"original equipment", the stuff the adventurer
brought into the dungeon, with the intention of
poising a new NetHack decision requirement: keep the
original equipment, knowing it will improve with
cartage/use, or ditch it for the immediate benefits
of some equipment without that "practice effect"
advantage. That, in turn, was intended to
differentiate the classes, since some start with a
lot of original equipment it would be nice to retain
for the sake of benefiting from a "practice effect",
some classes start with almost nothing worth
retaining, and (as far as I know) no two classes'
"original equipment" is identical.

This would also cater for downgrading some "original
equipment" that's probably a bit overpowered at
present, like the archeologist's touchstone or the
healer's stethescope, to "works with some
probability (and fails/lies the rest of the time)"
rather than "works always", and then slowly
upgrading the probability of success from your
"practice effect".

